<p>The United Arab Emirates said Tuesday it will leave OPEC and the wider OPEC+ alliance on May 1, ending almost six decades in the oil producers&#x27; group and removing one of its largest Gulf members. UAE officials said the decision followed a review of production policy, current and future capacity, and national interests; state media said the move reflected the country&#x27;s long-term strategic and economic vision and changing energy profile. The announcement followed years of Emirati objections to production quotas that officials said limited exports despite expanded capacity, as well as the Iran war&#x27;s disruption of Gulf energy shipments through the Strait of Hormuz. UAE Energy Minister Suhail Mohamed al-Mazrouei told Reuters the government did not discuss the decision with Saudi Arabia or any other country, and said shipping constraints in the strait meant the move would not have a huge market impact.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/OBZmIZsULMIdRrsJaR-pntA-OHgMyq1pwM4agz2bf0WxqTPgKatlclo6uAWa-O6qjvkkw2OSGN16IaNBX1lMUtSuVEU-njWVkMtO5OVjrHnr7AZoZMWVkyLFWks4FL8SNX6G0Zo-_v7Wl9WdpgPpoGodVBX8JpyKxrL13CTmaP5SVM0C2Cofo4EEI1dtcYC7QY3PXSnL39wtRQOQAFUswq6j2SqR0gMct8-fUHnPl_pgaQuoKemo4pvX0WlMBwqXR4Y_GMB0c3AtNJJCWsHqxvnJNyzDAiUKDrVJpneLv7EOCa0dfw1xVNHE4afrA_7Vr0us18DdKuLU1ufeNzfuptVgh4ftcTIYmv5dp7-kwf5e2pQRadGD8EDWLcPIoHXQjWJyqZC6btFuHW7YTknSfYKihT_WJD_W5oofweQMtlDrSKCnE8sfPWENGFj-JPImpOY5QKJaaKeXbIdZrlrGBR-8PleghC1jBb8' alt='Image distributed with coverage of the UAE&#x27;s OPEC exit.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Quota dispute: Abu Dhabi objected in 2021 to a production-cut extension because its quota baseline used 2018 output figures, before later investment expanded UAE capacity.</li><li>Capacity gap: OPEC granted the UAE a 3.5 million-barrel-per-day baseline in June 2024, while ADNOC has lifted capacity to 4.85 million bpd and targets 5 million bpd by 2030.</li><li>Demand outlook: WAM said underlying trends point to sustained global energy-demand growth over the medium to long term, despite near-term volatility in Gulf supply dynamics.</li></ul><blockquote>We thank OPEC and its member countries for decades of constructive cooperation - Suhail Mohamed al-Mazrouei</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>UAE Energy Ministry: The ministry said leaving the producer groups would let the UAE respond to changing market needs while continuing to contribute to stability in a measured way. (<a href='http://www.euronews.com/2026/04/28/uae-decides-to-leave-opec-citing-focus-on-its-national-interest'>Euronews</a>)</li><li>The Guardian analysis: The paper described the decision as political as well as commercial, saying it could revive UAE-Saudi tensions and strengthen Washington&#x27;s position in the region. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/apr/28/how-uae-leave-opec-recast-middle-east-saudi-arabia-us'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>Capital Economics: The research firm wrote that ties among OPEC members had loosened, particularly after Qatar withdrew from the cartel in 2019. (<a href='https://www.fastcompany.com/91533685/opec-united-arab-emirates-leaving-cartel'>Fast Company</a>)</li><li>Semafor analysis: Wael Mahdi argued the exit reflects a calculation that the cost of compliance in forgone production, revenue and investment had exceeded the benefits of membership. (<a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/04/28/2026/the-uaes-opec-exit-is-no-surprise'>Semafor</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/04/28/2026/the-uaes-opec-exit-is-no-surprise'>The UAE’s OPEC exit is no surprise</a> - semafor.com</li><li><a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/united-arab-emirates-quits-opec-in-blow-to-oil-cartel/'>United Arab Emirates quits OPEC in blow to oil cartel</a> - michaelwest.com.au</li><li><a href='https://www.allsides.com/news/2026-04-28-0700/middle-east-uae-leave-opec-amid-hormuz-oil-crisis-blow-saudi-arabia'>UAE to leave OPEC amid Hormuz oil crisis, a blow to Saudi Arabia<